<p id="p1">As part of yesterday’s 27.3 Developer Beta 2 releases, Apple introduced changes to Full Disk Access that <a href="https://www.macstories.net/linked/apple-announces-plan-to-impose-new-full-disk-access-controls-on-mac-developers/" rel="noopener noreferrer">were first announced last month</a>. As <a href="https://www.macstories.net/news/apples-latest-second-27-3-developer-beta-adds-face-unlock-for-iphone-duo-new-mac-privacy-controls-and-more/" rel="noopener noreferrer">John explained in his overview</a>, these changes revolve around new dialogs that explain the inherent risks of Full Disk Access to users as well as new controls to only grant access to specific apps and folders, which are part of the new ‘System &amp; App Protection Override’ settings.</p>
<p id="p2">In a surprising move for an early beta, Apple <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/149699" rel="noopener noreferrer">has already published a detailed support document</a> about these changes coming to macOS. From the document:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote3"><p>
  Starting in macOS 27.3, when some apps ask for Full Disk Access or System &amp; App Protection Override, you have the option to choose only specific protected folders or apps for the app to access. This puts you in control of what data is accessible to the app.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p4">And:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote5"><p>
  When you give an app System &amp; App Protection Override, you can choose specific apps for it to control. If you allow it to bypass system protections instead, it can fully control your Mac and apps, which allows it to access all of your data. It can also see every window and app, including ones in the background, and capture every keystroke.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p6">Not pictured in the support document is the dialog that macOS will bring up when you try to grant Full Disk Access to an app, also <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/16/macos-27-3-full-desk-access/" rel="noopener noreferrer">reported by MacRumors</a>:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/17/shortcut-upload-1794921953832-1f33e44bed.png" alt="Original screenshot via [@madeby_ldt](https://x.com/madeby_ldt/status/2122446115802935691)."><p class="image-caption">Original screenshot via <a href="https://x.com/madeby_ldt/status/2122446115802935691" rel="noopener noreferrer noopener noreferrer">@madeby_ldt</a>.</p></div>
<p id="p8">But there’s more: macOS will also show a persistent list of apps in the menu bar that have been granted Full Disk Access, as <a href="https://x.com/mweinbach/status/2122429363727831379" rel="noopener noreferrer">shown by Max Weinbach</a>:</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/17/shortcut-upload-1794922095101-d96f49ffc8.png" alt=""><p class="image-caption"></p></div>
<p id="p10">As <a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/11/16/macos-27-3-includes-new-privacy-controls-for-full-disk-access-and-more/" rel="noopener noreferrer">reported by Chance Miller at 9to5Mac</a>, macOS 27.3 will also show a menu bar item for apps that are controlling others via Automation as part of System &amp; App Protection Override. Apparently, <a href="https://x.com/apollozac/status/2122449099362378059" rel="noopener noreferrer">there is a Terminal command already</a> to turn this off.</p>
<p id="p11">On one hand, I think there’s a legitimate argument for Apple to get spooked by the fact that users may have been granting Full Disk Access to agentic assistants (let’s face it – this is what it’s mostly about) without fully realizing what that means. On the other, I am somewhat sympathetic to the <a href="https://x.com/markgurman/status/2122464242754552100" rel="noopener noreferrer">argument</a> that it’s convenient for Apple to dissuade users from granting Full Disk Access to third-party agents when its own Siri AI also has FDA-level capabilities without actually going through the same permission flow (or appearing in System Settings’ Full Disk Access panel at all).</p>
<p id="p12">But: if we agree that it’s within Apple’s rights to design security labels to educate its customers however they see fit, and if Full Disk Access <strong>isn’t actually going away</strong>…isn’t this system better and more flexible than before for more people?</p>
<p id="p13">Power users like me and you will still be able to give Codex or Claude Full Disk Access and we’ll happily click through Apple’s new scare sheets. Meanwhile, everyone else will be able to better understand what this kind of access entails; if they don’t feel comfortable giving Full Disk Access to an app, they’ll have a native, Apple-controlled option to scope it to specific folders.</p>
<p id="p14">If anything, these fine-grained settings may even <em>increase</em> the adoption of third-party agentic apps: users who don’t implicitly trust the likes of Meta or OpenAI will have a native feature to restrict those apps to specific locations on their Macs, while still being able to use those apps. That is – assuming companies like Meta and OpenAI agree to support Apple’s new Full Disk Access settings.</p>
<p id="p15"><a href="https://www.macstories.net/podcasts/appstories/505/" rel="noopener noreferrer">I was afraid</a> that Apple was going to kill Full Disk Access. As it turns out, this appears to be a non-issue and, in fact, a better system than before. We just need to click through a few additional buttons.</p>
